---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const pageUrl = 'https://www.ecchips.com/about';
const pageDescription =
  'ECCHIPS is an independent, free tool site for electronics engineers. Learn how SMD Code Lookup results are calculated and the principles behind the project.';

const webPageSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebPage',
  name: 'About — ECCHIPS',
  url: pageUrl,
  description: pageDescription,
  inLanguage: 'en',
  isPartOf: { '@type': 'WebSite', name: 'ECCHIPS', url: 'https://www.ecchips.com' }
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About ECCHIPS – Independent Tools for Electronics Engineers"
  description={pageDescription}
  canonical="/about"
  jsonLd={[webPageSchema]}
>
  <section class="page">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>About</h1>

      <p>
        ECCHIPS is an independent project that builds small, focused tools for
        electronics engineers. The first tool is the
        <a href="/smd-code-lookup">SMD Code Lookup</a>: a fast way to decode
        common SMD markings into possible resistor or ceramic capacitor values.
      </p>

      <h2>Current scope</h2>
      <p>
        Version 1.0 deliberately does one thing only: decoding three-digit,
        four-digit and EIA-96 markings. There is no component database, no
        account system, no backend and no advertising. If a feature does not
        help you decode a marking faster, it is not in this version.
      </p>

      <h2>How results are calculated</h2>
      <p>
        Results are produced by deterministic marking rules implemented
        directly by ECCHIPS, computed locally in your browser. ECCHIPS does not
        copy or resell any third-party parts database, and no query leaves your
        device. Because the same calculation rules run on every request, the
        same input always produces the same output.
      </p>

      <h2>Accuracy principles</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Markings follow commonly used conventions, not universal laws.
          Descriptions on this site use careful wording for that reason.
        </li>
        <li>
          A three-digit code is reported as ambiguous by design: it may encode
          a resistor value or a ceramic capacitor value, and the marking alone
          cannot tell you which.
        </li>
        <li>
          When a code cannot be decoded under a supported rule, the tool says
          so instead of guessing.
        </li>
        <li>
          Always confirm against the component's datasheet and your own
          measurements before relying on a decoded value in real hardware.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Independence and limitations</h2>
      <p>
        ECCHIPS is not affiliated with any component manufacturer or
        distributor. It is an educational reference tool: it interprets
        markings, it does not identify individual physical components, verify
        authenticity, or guarantee tolerance ratings.
      </p>

      <h2>Privacy</h2>
      <p>
        No account, no upload, no tracking by default. Recent lookups are kept
        only in your browser's local storage. See the
        <a href="/privacy">Privacy</a> page for details.
      </p>

      <h2>Roadmap</h2>
      <p>
        More single-purpose engineering tools are planned, added one at a time
        once each proves useful. Suggestions and correction reports from
        working engineers are the main input for what gets built next.
      </p>

      {/* TODO(owner): add a real contact channel (email or form) here before launch.
          Do not publish an address that is not monitored. */}

      <p class="page-updated">Last updated: August 26, 2026</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
